diff --git a/docs/managed-reviews-v1.md b/docs/managed-reviews-v1.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0a4b043
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/managed-reviews-v1.md
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+# Managed reviews: Source JSON2, default off
+
+This change starts from Povédai `4cb52f521be72d6b9e2ec3eff0ab7fb95ede6646`. It adds an optional Source-owned managed namespace to the existing JSON document. It does not add a Root database, alter Prisma/PostgreSQL, replace native login, or modify `review-widget.js` / public API 1.1.
+
+## Identity and authority
+
+`createManagedReviews(store, { enabled, verifier, nativeVerifier?, timeoutMs? })` accepts reviewed host code. Its `verifier.verify(credential, signal)` must return the exact configured RP identity after maintained issuer/signature/audience/nonce verification and fresh issuer session/userinfo validation. Incoming JSON claims, descriptors, email, phone, display name and Root agent grants cannot instantiate this port. No ID/access/refresh/native session token is stored in JSON2.
+
+The persistent Source principal is an exact, case-sensitive `(issuer, sub)` binding and a private label. It is not an `AdminUser` or a platform/site role. Root's private profile name is not copied to public reviews: the default alias is «Участник»; `authorLabel` is a separate explicit text choice and grants no identity assurance. All managed reviews start `pending`; the native renderer continues to escape content. No passkey/hardware/person-assurance claim is introduced.
+
+`service.operator.grant()` is Source administrative composition only, not an HTTP/MCP operation. It approves one principal, exact `{registryId, tenantId, appId, environmentId}`, 1–3 case-sensitive app/project/person resources, rights and a keyId/generation with at most 24 hours remaining. The Source record, active flag and expiry are checked in the atomic native commit and again before results. Revoking a key permanently deactivates its grants; inactive key IDs and grant IDs cannot be rewritten/reactivated. A new explicitly approved key/grant is required. There is no permission inference from title, domain, metadata or a Root author draft.
+
+The existing native subject/owner placement is provisioned in one commit with its binding and receipt. New managed resources have `sharingMode:private`, `canDisplay:false`, and public collection/reply/moderation flags false. Public metadata, direct object/subject, aliases, counts and recent/read projections are fenced. `publish` requires the explicit Source publish right and binding owner; moderation requires the separate moderate right. No native demo site, admin, password or email is auto-created in a managed tenant.
+
+`collect`/`reply` reuse the native review constructor and event stream, including exact parent/object checks and bounded nested depth. `moderate` reuses the native status/event constructor. The private `list` result gives a moderator its approved resource's rows; a participant with collect/reply rights receives only its native author histories. Identity claims and credentials are excluded. This slice does not silently adopt an old public subject or merge native accounts. Existing public-read mappings remain usable; managed adoption of existing sites/objects and publication-policy UX needs a separate explicit owner workflow.
+
+`attachLegacy` requires both the Root principal and a current Source native proof. `createManagedNativeVerifier` reuses actual `AdminAuthService.session` or the native site-bound author token / ownership hashes, without creating a matching author by email. Links are additive and one-to-one, never rewrite old IDs, password hashes or review history, and grant no additional native role. Native admin attach records an identity link; it does not add a new site membership. A revoked native token/session cannot be used for repeated attachment.
+
+## Storage, replay and failure
+
+`AtomicJsonDocument` is used by every native/managed JSON reader/writer: one exclusive lock, a bounded current snapshot, a synchronous domain callback, exclusive temporary file, file fsync, atomic replace, directory fsync on Unix. Windows supports file fsync/replace here, without the Unix directory durability guarantee. Windows lock-creation EPERM/delete-pending contention is retried only before the callback; callbacks, identity requests, effects and token exchanges are never retried automatically.
+
+The lock has PID+nonce and inode/device witnesses. A crash retains it. There is no timeout-based stale takeover. `recoverDeadOwner` requires an exact witness and ESRCH twice-checked identity with writers/recovery tools quiesced; live/reused PIDs refuse. A death before replace preserves prior bytes. A lost acknowledgement after replace is reconciled by the same reviewed operation key and exact intent; altered intent conflicts. Post-commit Root/Source revocation can deny the acknowledgement while the Source receipt already exists. This is a Source atomic commit, not a distributed Root/issuer/runtime transaction.
+
+Limits: document 128 MiB (a lower trusted limit is allowed), 256 principals, 512 identity links, 256 grants, 384 bindings, 10,000 operation receipts, 4 actual outstanding issuer/native verification promises per service, 15-second actor scope, bounded 3-second default verifier wait. Abort-ignoring verification keeps its slot until it settles. There is no quota eviction, private content cache or silent authority/history rewrite. A full authority/receipt store fails closed. A caller can explicitly reconcile an ambiguous request; the adapter never performs an automatic duplicate Source operation.
+
+## Reader-first migration and fallback
+
+1. With all writers quiesced, preserve an encrypted coherent backup including native/runtime custody separately as applicable. Inspect a coherent read-only snapshot with the independent literal reader (`managed-store-probe-cli --file <approved snapshot>`); output is only public protocol/version and counts. Bind actual reviewed Source image capability to reader versions. A label/file declaration alone is not that runtime check.
+2. Deploy the reviewed reader2/atomic-writer baseline with managed service absent/disabled and constructor `allowManagedMigration:false`. It reads JSON1/JSON2 and preserves the complete managed namespace during native writes. Do not run old JSON writers concurrently.
+3. Only after that baseline is an independently verified fallback, enable the reviewed Source RP/service and explicitly pass `allowManagedMigration:true` in the trusted constructor for the first managed commit. No HTTP argument or default config enables migration. Migration is additive: native IDs/history are retained and JSON2 cannot be downgraded to JSON1.
+4. Feature-off fallback uses reader2 and refuses new managed admission while retaining JSON2 and native operation. Old4cb normalizes away unknown collections; it does **not** self-refuse JSON2. The external pre-START reader gate must reject old4cb/reader1 on a JSON2 volume. Never START it to test this on user data.
+
+Actual Linux images, file/owner permissions, cold reader invocation and coherent backup/restore are still required before deploying this change. Local Windows tests prove process contention, byte preservation and explicit recovery; they do not prove a Linux image or disk-loss durability. Managed PostgreSQL returns `managed_postgres_unavailable` until an additive Source migration, independent reader/fallback and actual PG tests exist. Native PostgreSQL is unchanged.
+
+## Composition seams and current unsupported paths
+
+Source `createApplication({ ..., managedReviews })` optionally registers `POST /api/managed/v1/{provision,context,list,collect,publish,moderate,attach}`. No operator/grant HTTP route exists. Mutation/read POSTs require the configured exact Source origin and the trusted RP verifier; default composition returns managed503 and writes no namespace. This patch does not add a production OIDC client/BFF loader, Source refresh/key custody, browser consent/onboarding UI, a network Root transport, old managed-object adoption, or an agent capability/budget registration. These are concrete next integration gates, not supplied by a descriptor. The existing native publication/consent UX and renderer remain unchanged; managed publication UX must review Source policy before exposing this optional path to visitors.
+
+Root `createManagedReviewsPort` captures actual Apps/Root authority synchronously, calls the verified Source between locks, then checks current authority. The host resolves transient Source credentials from an explicitly approved private RP/account binding. It owns no DB, issues no Source grant, accepts no arbitrary URL/script/key, and makes no whole-SDK/readiness claim. A Source receipt can produce exact scoped subject pins, not executable-code attestation or automatic Registration approval. HTTP/MCP agent dispatch requires its normal separately approved capability and budget plumbing.
+
+## Reproducible synthetic checks
+
+Run from this Source checkout after its own dependencies and Vite client assets are present, with `TEST_DATABASE_URL`, `DATABASE_URL`, `REDIS_URL` absent/empty:
+
+```text
+node node_modules/typescript/bin/tsc --noEmit
+node node_modules/typescript/bin/tsc -p tsconfig.server.json
+node node_modules/vite/bin/vite.js build
+node --import tsx --test --test-concurrency=1 tests/*.test.ts
+```
+
+Run Root pure reviews checks from its checkout: `node --test modules/reviews/test/*.test.mjs`.
+
+The actual Root+Source fixture is outside Root's ordinary `.mjs` glob because Source is separately typed/versioned. Run **from the Source checkout** (its static-assets cwd contract is preserved):
+
+```text
+node --import tsx --test <Root checkout>/modules/reviews/fixtures/managed-source-http.test.mjs
+```
+
+All accounts, grants, keys, runtime/native data and HTTP providers in those gates are synthetic loopback/task-owned fixtures. Production accounts/configuration/queues are never read or changed. PostgreSQL/Docker gates are not substituted by these fixtures.
